<Article
  title="You already have a will back home. Does it protect your UAE assets?"
  description="A will from your home country may not work automatically in the UAE. What validation really involves — and why the clean answer is one will per jurisdiction."
  image="/images/article-passport.jpg"
  minutes={5}
>
  <p>
    "I already sorted my will in Italy / the UK / India — I'm covered, right?"
    It is the most common assumption we hear, and it is understandable. You did
    the responsible thing once; doing it again feels redundant.
  </p>
  <p>
    The honest answer: your home-country will is valuable — for your assets
    <strong>there</strong>. For your UAE assets, it may not work automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>What "may not work automatically" actually means</h2>
  <p>
    A foreign will can potentially be validated for use in the UAE — but
    validation is a process, not a formality. It can involve:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Notarisation</strong> in the country where the will was made;</li>
    <li><strong>Attestation</strong> through the relevant authorities;</li>
    <li><strong>Certified Arabic translation</strong> by an accredited translator;</li>
    <li><strong>Court approval</strong> in the UAE.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each step has its own requirements and queue. Together, the process
    <strong>could take 6 to 12 months</strong> — and the outcome is not
    guaranteed to match the original document's intent once translated and
    reviewed.
  </p>

  <h2>While validation runs, your assets could stay locked</h2>
  <p>
    During those months, the usual defaults apply: bank accounts could be
    frozen (joint accounts included), and property typically cannot be sold or
    rented out until the court completes the transfer. Your family would be
    managing a foreign validation process — in a language and a system they may
    not know — at the hardest possible time.
  </p>

  <h2>The clean answer: one will per jurisdiction</h2>
  <p>
    The widely accepted solution is simple: <strong>one will for each
    country</strong> where you hold meaningful assets.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your home-country will covers your assets back home, under the rules it was written for.</li>
    <li>Your UAE will covers your UAE assets, written for the registry where it is filed.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The two documents complement each other — they do not conflict. Each one
    speaks the language (literally and legally) of the system that will use it.
  </p>

  <h2>Less effort than you think</h2>
  <p>
    A UAE will takes 2 to 3 hours of your personal time, done remotely: one
    guided session, a draft review, and a short government video call in
    English. Your home will stays exactly as it is — you are simply completing
    the picture.
  </p>
</Article>
